  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Trevor McDonald shut down Arizona for six scoreless innings and the offense delivered when it mattered, breaking a two-game slide with a 6-4 road win.",
    "what_this_means": "McDonald's shutdown changed everything. Six innings, one hit, five strikeouts. He gave them a margin to work with and they used it. The lineup didn't need much. Heliot Ramos and Victor Bericoto each drove in two runs, and the bats found enough in the fifth and sixth to build a 6-0 lead. Arizona scored four in the eighth to make it ugly, but it didn't matter. This is what balance looks like: pitching that holds the line and hitting that converts runners on base. They're one game back into a rhythm after dropping two straight. The formula works when the starter buys time and the bats cash it.",
